{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792502845,"nonce":"/UeZgdkY0EvkVy3FmyeLiJnGLbE=","message":"Scope refinement after auditing the shared module and snapshot implementation\n\nThis is TypeScript integration work over existing Zyntax infrastructure. Do not build a second linker, import cache or snapshot format. Initial concrete-function import wiring is a medium integration step; the full TS-04 scope remains large because ECMAScript module semantics and exported type metadata still need frontend work.\n\nReuse already present:\n- typed_ast/import_resolver.rs: ModuleArchitecture::typescript(), ImportContext, ImportResolver/ImportManager, aliases and module/export descriptors. The TypeScript architecture already generates direct-file and index candidates; do not mistake those candidates for complete modern TypeScript/Node package-resolution semantics.\n- zyntax_embed/import_chain.rs: source/CompiledImport resolver chains, recursive declaration/type-registry merging, module origins and language-qualified snapshot lookup. A native OXC frontend should supply CompiledImport results; the current source callback path tries registered LanguageGrammar parsers and is not an OXC parser hook.\n- CompiledImport serializes the TypedProgram plus its TypeRegistry; SnapshotBuilder::module_lowered stores interfaces/signature tables plus split HIR. Snapshot::module_interface/function_signature can answer typing queries without decoding all function bodies.\n- lower_for_snapshot_releasing precomputes optimized HIR and release facts. SnapshotBuilder::module_lowered, include_snapshot and TieredRuntime::install_snapshot already provide library packaging, type-ID reservation and installation. No grammar is required for a native frontend.\n- Snapshot/LazyModule share encoded bodies by Arc and decode on demand. The closed TieredRuntime path uses selective imports and deferred bodies. zynode already declares ts$main and calls enter_only_through_entry_points, so preserve that path. This is serialized typed/HIR program data, not a persisted heap or a native JIT-code image; interpreter-first execution and later JIT tier-up remain the runtime's job.\n\nTypeScript work that remains:\n1. Build the exported checker interface before lowering importers: aliases, readonly/source property views, generic parameters, physical layouts, ownership and array result/provenance summaries. Several of these currently exist only in Lowerer maps, so serializing the current TypedProgram alone is not a complete TS export interface. Cache by canonical module identity and preserve interned names. Give each module unique native function and initializer identities instead of reusing ts$main/ts$fn$name across files.\n2. Lower named/default/namespace/type-only imports, export aliases and re-exports to shared import/module metadata. Keep ESM live bindings, once-only evaluation, side-effect imports and cycle/temporal-dead-zone semantics explicit. Shared recursive-load suppression is not ESM execution semantics. Repeated selective imports from the same module need their requested signatures combined, because the current shared chain marks a module processed after the first request.\n3. Package stable TS libraries/user modules with the shared snapshot builder, matching runtime automatic_release, strict_fp and frontend policy. Audit hard-coded snapshot-lowering defaults: the TS runtime disables pattern rewrites, while lower_for_snapshot_releasing currently enables them. Reserve snapshot type IDs before frontend type allocation; the present CLI parses before register_runtime, so an installer added only there would be too late for that guarantee.\n4. Keep snapshots selective and body-borrowing. Do not call Snapshot::modules/LazyModule::full on the startup path or deep-clone every dependency. Keep each module initializer reachable even when its exported functions are unused; type-only imports must not run it.\n5. For persistent on-disk module snapshots, include source/transitive dependency hashes, TS frontend/options/resolution policy, native compiler/build/schema/target ABI and builtin ABI identities in invalidation. Reuse loader checks and ID relocation; do not treat compiler BUILD_ID alone as a complete TS source cache key. Measure parse/check/lower, snapshot load, first call and process wall time separately so deferred work is not reported as eliminated.\n\nConcrete shared prerequisite found:\n4a87f8d3fe17ac9c076fd9b74eb268ee62a4489469313631b84dbde30376ecea — retain exported generic templates. The current snapshot-lowering pipeline calls monomorphize_module, which removes generic originals and restores only concrete instances; module_lowered then strips typed bodies. Verify a separately packaged first\u003cT\u003e(xs:T[],fallback:T) can still specialize in the importing runtime for numeric/boolean/string elements, with cached instances and typed nonzero-index GEPs. This prerequisite is based on code inspection; current concrete-function snapshot tests do not cover it.\n\nExisting related issues, reused rather than duplicated:\n- 5e24efcb4fb48c15ac0ad0a57dee42ab4308d831fc0a4e8ea988709a4e5bf18f — mismatched lowered snapshot must fail explicitly. module_lowered has stripped bodies, so returning declarations without usable HIR is not a working fallback. Reparse/rebuild only from an explicit source path, otherwise diagnose.\n- ee1676c569fb61b6e1ce249d900cd204e8e0fb6f4ae92297f931bd4c612eebe9 — preserve frontend type metadata during registry rebuilds.\n- 35cd150f680dded61c271d4f40e3f3ae1db12c0d877987665cde33a3cb186160 — keep deferred snapshot decoding off timed first calls; account for first-use cost honestly.\n\nValidation of existing reuse paths: 13 snapshot unit tests, 4 import-chain language-selection tests, 6 prelowered-linking integration tests and the borrowed-body allocation regression all pass (24 tests total). These validate existing shared concrete-function infrastructure, not completed TS import/export support or the new generic-template prerequisite.\n\nExtend acceptance with source-versus-snapshot equivalence, transitive and repeated selective imports, type-only/re-export behavior, side effects/cycles/live bindings, metadata/readonly retention, cached generic instantiations, stale/mismatched-cache diagnostics, and actual interpreter/Cranelift/LLVM execution. Preserve direct array GEPs and static releases across module boundaries.","files":null}]}